WEBVTT

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:27.680
<v Ira Glass>As a species, something drives us to want to make inanimate things seem alive. There are statues where the whole idea seems to be to create a simulation of soft skin and sheer billowing cloth out of rock-- literally, out of a rock-- as if to prove this, too, can be human. There is just something about that moment when Pinocchio stops being a few strung together pieces of wood and starts to seem like he's really a little boy.
<v Ira Glass>作为一个物种，我们内心有一种冲动，想要让无生命的事物看起来栩栩如生。有些雕像，其创作初衷似乎就是用岩石——真的是用岩石——创造出柔软皮肤和飘逸布料的模拟效果，仿佛在证明这也可以是人性的一部分。当匹诺曹不再只是几块绑在一起的木头，而开始看起来像个真正的小男孩时，那一刻总是让人感慨万千。

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:42.980
<v Ira Glass>This week I caught a guy named Ronn Lucas, a ventriloquist who is best known for being able to make anything talk-- a banana, a box of breakfast cereal, a sock-- to ask him what he makes of all this. His dummies are named Buffalo Billy and Scorch.
<v Ira Glass>本周我采访了罗恩·卢卡斯，他是一位口技表演者，最擅长让任何东西开口说话——香蕉、一盒麦片、一只袜子——我想问问他是如何看待这一点的。他的木偶名字叫“水牛比利”和“焦灼”。

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:50.860
<v Ronn Lucas>When I'm on stage talking to my puppets, I believe they're real. I believe to the point, as an actor believes in his character.
<v Ronn Lucas>当我在台上和木偶说话时，我相信它们是真实的。我相信这一点，就像演员相信自己的角色一样。

00:00:50.860 --> 00:01:03.790
<v Ira Glass>Charles Ludlam, of the Ridiculous Theater, had a dummy called Walter. And he wrote at one point about Walter, he sometimes says unexpected things. Does that happen with you, with Billy or Scorch?
<v Ira Glass>“荒诞剧团”的查尔斯·勒德拉姆有一个叫沃尔特的木偶。他曾写道，沃尔特有时会说出一些意想不到的话。你和比利或焦灼之间发生过这种情况吗？

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:19.510
<v Ronn Lucas>It has happened to me. There are a couple moments, too. I started to get through a breakthrough as a comedian. I was working a series of comedy clubs. And someone asked me if I would do a military club. And I said, yeah, sure, not knowing what I was getting into. I worked for the enlisted men. And they were just really into what I was doing.
<v Ronn Lucas>我确实遇到过。有几次，也是我作为喜剧演员取得突破的时候。当时我在一系列喜剧俱乐部工作。有人问我是否愿意去一个军人俱乐部表演。我说，好啊，没问题，当时根本不知道自己要面对什么。我为服役士兵表演，他们非常喜欢我的演出。

00:01:19.510 --> 00:01:43.820
<v Ronn Lucas>But then I went over to the officer's club. And these guys were wild. I guess it's part of the responsibility of command, is that when you get to hang out just with other officers, you can let yourself go. But performing, I couldn't do my script. I couldn't do the show I had planned. So I just started doing question and answer stuff. Don Rickles, ethnic jokes, nothing that would really hurt anybody. But I was picking on everybody equally.
<v Ronn Lucas>但后来我去了军官俱乐部。这些家伙简直太狂野了。我想这是指挥职责的一部分，当你只和军官们待在一起时，你可以彻底放松。但表演时，我无法照本宣科，无法按照预先计划的节目表演。所以我只是开始进行问答互动。唐·里克斯式的、关于民族的笑话，没伤害到任何人，但我对每个人都一视同仁地进行了调侃。

00:01:43.820 --> 00:02:04.890
<v Ronn Lucas>I had to preface that before I got to where I-- So one guy was an officer. And he was from Puerto Rico. I said hey, where are you from? And the guy said, Puerto Rico. And the dummy goes, how many Puerto Ricans does it take to screw in a light bulb? And I'm thinking to myself, why did I have him say that? I don't have an answer.
<v Ronn Lucas>在我进入正题之前，我得先做个铺垫——当时有一个军官。他是波多黎各人。我说嘿，你从哪儿来？那家伙说，波多黎各。然后木偶就说，拧一个灯泡需要多少个波多黎各人？我心想，我为什么要让他说这个？我根本不知道答案。

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:22.120
<v Ira Glass>Then, in a wink, the dummy came up with an answer. One funny enough to make an audience laugh in an officer's nightclub. But I have to say, not so funny that I want to alienate our lovely multicultural audience.
<v Ira Glass>然后，在眨眼之间，木偶想出了一个答案。那个答案足够幽默，逗得军官夜总会的观众哈哈大笑。但我必须说，还没幽默到让我想要疏远我们可爱的多元文化观众的程度。

00:02:22.120 --> 00:02:29.250
<v Ira Glass>And why is it so pleasurable for something inanimate to suddenly become alive through a voice coming out of it?
<v Ira Glass>为什么让无生命的东西通过发出的声音突然变得鲜活起来，会让人感到如此愉悦呢？

00:02:29.250 --> 00:02:39.870
<v Ronn Lucas>It is fascinating to hold a fork in your hand and wonder what it would say. I found myself doing that not too long ago around a table with some other ventriloquists.
<v Ronn Lucas>手里拿着一把叉子，好奇它会说什么，这真是一件迷人的事。不久前，我和其他几位口技表演者围坐在一张桌子旁时，我发现自己也做了同样的事情。

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.070
<v Ira Glass>And what did it say?
<v Ira Glass>它说了什么？

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.320
<v Ronn Lucas>And it said, your thumb is on my butt.
<v Ronn Lucas>它说，你的大拇指放在我的屁股上了。

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.080
<v Ira Glass>Right. Of course. Exactly.
<v Ira Glass>对。当然。一点没错。

00:02:48.080 --> 00:03:01.210
<v Ronn Lucas>I treated it more like a person. I didn't think of it as a utility, or what it was going to do. I saw it as a human form. And my thumb was in the wrong place.
<v Ronn Lucas>我把它当成了一个人。我没把它看作工具或它应该完成的任务，我把它看作一种人形。而我的大拇指放错位置了。

00:03:01.210 --> 00:03:21.900
<v Ira Glass>Which is exactly the point. Every object Ronn Lucas makes speak, he pretends is a person. This is exactly what we're doing as a species all the time. Turning things into people. And it begins when we're children, playing with dolls. That's true for men and women. Today on our program, what that's all about.
<v Ira Glass>这正是重点所在。罗恩·卢卡斯让每个开口说话的物体，都假装是一个人。这正是我们作为一个物种一直在做的事情。把事物变成人。这始于我们童年玩洋娃娃的时候。无论是男人还是女人都是如此。今天，我们就来探讨这其中的奥秘。

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:37.070
<v Ira Glass>From WBEZ Chicago and Public Radio International, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's show, stories of dolls, dolls of all sorts, and what is it all about?
<v Ira Glass>这里是芝加哥WBEZ和国际公共广播电台联合播出的《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目，关于玩偶的故事，各种各样的玩偶，这一切究竟是怎么回事？

00:03:37.070 --> 00:04:22.310
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Thank Heaven for Little Girls-- Made of Plastic. The story of a company that has figured out all the different ways that girls like dolls, and is trying to sell to nearly every single way they can think of. Act Two, You Know What Mr. Bear Would Say. The author of a once-famous series of children's books about a doll, and how her life came to resemble a doll's. Act Three, The Boy Who Would Be a Helicopter. Kindergarten teacher Vivian Paley tells the story of how, in this age when schools are eliminating the doll corner at younger and younger ages, it was playing with dolls that saved a kid in her class. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，感谢上帝创造了小女孩——还有塑料制品。一家公司摸清了女孩们喜欢玩偶的所有方式，并试图通过他们能想到的几乎每一种方式进行销售。第二幕，你知道熊先生会说什么。一位曾经著名的儿童书系列作家关于一个玩偶的故事，以及她的人生是如何变得像一个玩偶的。第三幕，想成为直升机的男孩。幼儿园老师薇薇安·佩利讲述了在一个学校越来越早地撤掉玩偶角的年代，玩耍玩偶是如何拯救了她班上的一个孩子。请继续收听。

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:48.330
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Thank Heaven for Little Girls-- Made of Plastic. About a year ago, here in Chicago, just around the corner from all the fanciest stores that you can find for a three state radius-- Niketown, and the Sony Gallery, and Neiman Marcus, and all those kind-- a company began an experiment in marketing dolls to little girls. They opened a store, the only one that they have opened anywhere. This American Life producer Susan Burton headed down to American Girl Place.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，感谢上帝创造了小女孩——还有塑料制品。大约一年前，在芝加哥，就在方圆三个州内你能找到的所有最豪华商店的拐角处——耐克城、索尼画廊、尼曼百货，以及所有这类商店——一家公司开始了一项向小女孩营销玩偶的实验。他们开了一家店，这是他们在任何地方开的唯一一家店。《美国生活》制作人苏珊·伯顿前往了“美国女孩广场”。

00:04:48.330 --> 00:05:03.580
<v Susan Burton>It's American Girl Place, not American Girl store. The word place was what thrilled me before I had ever set foot inside. It seemed to connote a project of such grand scale. Build a monument on the Magnificent Mile, and they will come.
<v Susan Burton>是“美国女孩广场”，而不是“美国女孩商店”。在我踏入大门之前，“广场”这个词就让我感到激动。它似乎暗示了一个宏大的项目。在“璀璨大道”上建立一座丰碑，他们就会蜂拥而至。

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:24.750
<v Susan Burton>You see them on Saturday afternoons downtown, swarms of little girls. What you notice first is their shopping bags, which are bright, Crayola marker red with American Girl Place stamped in white across the side. Austere, grown-up looking bags. Even if the bags are large, banging against their legs, the girls tend to carry them by themselves.
<v Susan Burton>周六下午在市中心，你会看到成群结队的小女孩。你首先注意到的是她们的购物袋，那是明亮的蜡笔红，侧面印着白色的“美国女孩广场”字样。严肃而成熟的包。即使袋子很大，撞击着她们的腿，女孩们也倾向于自己拎着。

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:39.900
<v Susan Burton>In good coats and tights and Mary Janes, they look like little girls pretending to be little girls. The question of what to wear to American Girl Place has clearly been given careful consideration. Some girls are dressed like their dolls.
<v Susan Burton>穿着好看的外套、连裤袜和玛丽珍鞋，她们看起来就像是在假装成为小女孩的小女孩。对于在美国女孩广场该穿什么，她们显然经过了仔细考虑。有些女孩的打扮和她们的玩偶一样。

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:55.410
<v Whitney Robertson>I have the purple 2000 millennium outfit. There are purple tights that  are sparkly and a purple skirt that goes down to about my knees. I have a purple tank top with a purple cardigan.
<v Whitney Robertson>我有紫色的2000年千禧装。有闪闪发光的紫色连裤袜和一条到我膝盖左右的紫色裙子。我有一件紫色吊带背心，配一件紫色开衫。

00:05:55.410 --> 00:06:09.540
<v Susan Burton>Whitney Robertson is 11 years old and came here from Saint Louis with her mother, Laura. She has short blond hair, braces. She moves with a heightened formality. It's as if she is wearing something magical and doesn't dare disturb the spell.
<v Susan Burton>惠特尼·罗伯逊今年11岁，和她的母亲劳拉从圣路易斯来到这里。她留着金色短发，戴着牙套。她的动作带着一种高度的正式感。就好像她穿着某种神奇的东西，不敢破坏这咒语。

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:18.620
<v Laura Robertson>The most interesting thing is when she got her braces this year at Christmas time, she had to have purple-- what do you call them, spacers?
<v Laura Robertson>最有趣的是，今年圣诞节她戴上牙套时，她必须戴上紫色的——那叫什么来着，分牙器？

00:06:18.620 --> 00:06:30.380
<v Whitney Robertson>The purple rubber bands. Because I got my braces a few days after Christmas, so I knew I got the 2000 outfit. So that's the only reason I got purple. Otherwise would have gotten my favorite color, blue.
<v Whitney Robertson>紫色的橡皮筋。因为我在圣诞节后几天戴的牙套，所以我知道我能拿到2000年的套装。这就是我选择紫色的唯一原因。否则我会选我最喜欢的蓝色。

00:06:30.380 --> 00:06:32.990
<v Laura Robertson>So she was totally American Girl.
<v Laura Robertson>所以她完全是一个“美国女孩”。

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:45.320
<v Susan Burton>Whitney and her mother have come to American Girl Place, not to Chicago, in the same way that you go to Disney World, rather than to Orlando. This is Whitney's first time here. Her parents gave her the trip for Christmas.
<v Susan Burton>惠特尼和她的母亲来到美国女孩广场，并不是为了来芝加哥，就像你去迪士尼乐园，而不是为了去奥兰多。这是惠特尼第一次来这里。她的父母把这次旅行作为圣诞礼物送给了她。

00:06:45.320 --> 00:07:01.630
<v Whitney Robertson>There was a series of presents. The first one was a necklace that said, northern expressions. And I really didn't get it at the time, but it was a clue there we go north from Saint Louis. And then the next one was something from AG. I think it was a necklace.
<v Whitney Robertson>这是一系列礼物。第一个是一个写着“北方表达”的项链。我当时真的没明白，但这显然是一个暗示，我们要从圣路易斯向北走。然后下一个是AG的礼物。我想是一条项链。

00:07:01.630 --> 00:07:03.930
<v Susan Burton>AG. American Girl.
<v Susan Burton>AG。美国女孩。

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.690
<v Whitney Robertson>And then the next one was airline tickets to the AG Place.
<v Whitney Robertson>然后下一个是去AG广场的机票。

00:07:06.690 --> 00:07:14.850
<v Laura Robertson>Whitney had this questioning look on her face, like, what? And then it dawned on her. And she just went, oh my gosh, and started to cry.
<v Laura Robertson>惠特尼脸上露出疑惑的表情，好像在说，什么？然后她恍然大悟。她只是说，哦，天哪，然后开始哭了起来。

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.400
<v Whitney Robertson>True.
<v Whitney Robertson>真的。

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:44.600
<v Susan Burton>In the store's first year, about 40% of it's million visitors have come from out of town. This is retail as theme park. Destination shopping. There is a concierge desk, coat checks on both the first and third floors. There is a cafe, and a clothing department, a museum, and a live musical, loads of furniture and accessories, shelves of souvenirs, and, of course, the dolls themselves, which cost $82 each.
<v Susan Burton>在商店开业的第一年，一百万游客中约有40%来自外地。这简直是零售业的主题公园。目的地购物。有一个礼宾台，一楼和三楼有衣帽间。有一家咖啡馆，一个服装部，一个博物馆，还有现场音乐剧，大量的家具和配饰，成排的纪念品，当然，还有玩偶本身，每个售价82美元。

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:59.890
<v Susan Burton>A former school teacher with a storybook name, Pleasant Rowland, thought up the American Girl dolls about 15 years ago. There are six historic dolls. Each represents a different era, and is accompanied by a series of chapter books about her life and times.
<v Susan Burton>一位有着故事书般名字的前学校老师，普莱森特·罗兰，大约15年前想出了“美国女孩”玩偶的创意。有六个历史玩偶。每一个代表一个不同的时代，并配有一系列关于她生活和时代的系列章节书。

00:07:59.890 --> 00:08:19.470
<v Susan Burton>Samantha, the most popular doll, is a Victorian girl. Molly misses her father, who is fighting in World War II. Addie escaped slavery and moved north to Philadelphia. The girls are spunky and independent, and the books are full of historical detail. Quiting bees, suffragettes, victory gardens.
<v Susan Burton>最受欢迎的玩偶萨曼莎是一个维多利亚时代的女孩。莫莉想念正在二战中作战的父亲。艾迪逃离了奴隶制，向北移居到费城。女孩们坚强且独立，书中充满了历史细节。缝纫聚会、妇女参政论者、胜利花园。

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:32.350
<v Susan Burton>About 5 million dolls have been sold since 1986, mostly by catalog. The company won't say to whom, but as a fourth grade teacher, Whitney's mother has noticed which girls tend to own the dolls.
<v Susan Burton>自1986年以来，已经售出了约500万个玩偶，主要通过目录销售。公司不愿透露客户是谁，但作为一名四年级教师，惠特尼的母亲注意到了哪些女孩倾向于拥有这些玩偶。

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:52.150
<v Laura Robertson>I teach in a poorer area outside of Saint Louis. Most of the people would be lower middle class. I would say about 5% of my 9 to 10-year-olds right now. Now, my daughter's school, who is in the more middle class, maybe upper middle class, area, probably a good 80%--
<v Laura Robertson>我在圣路易斯郊区的一个贫困地区教书。大多数人属于中下阶层。我可以说目前我班上9到10岁的孩子里大约有5%拥有。而在我女儿的学校，那个处于中产阶级，或许是中上阶层地区的学校，可能有80%——

00:08:52.150 --> 00:08:54.840
<v Whitney Robertson>80%. Out of the whole school.
<v Whitney Robertson>80%。整个学校。

00:08:54.840 --> 00:09:05.190
<v Laura Robertson>Out of the whole school. Yes. Because you'll see them wearing the t-shirts. You'll see them carrying the dolls. And they talk about it a lot. And it's a big thing.
<v Laura Robertson>整个学校。是的。因为你会看到她们穿着T恤。你会看到她们带着玩偶。她们经常谈论这个。这是一件大事。

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:21.280
<v Susan Burton>The crowd in the store seems well off. Mostly, but not entirely, white. One girl, who must be all of seven, with elegant upswept hair and a long burgundy coat, carries her wire shopping basket around all day with the authority of a duty free heiress.
<v Susan Burton>店里的人群看起来都很富裕。主要是白人，但并非全是。一个看起来顶多七岁、留着优雅盘发并穿着长款酒红色外套的女孩，整天拎着她的铁丝购物篮，散发着免税店女继承人的权威感。

00:09:21.280 --> 00:09:49.400
<v Susan Burton>Whitney didn't even like dolls, her mother tells me, until she got the first Samantha book three years ago. The history drew Whitney into the doll. And lots of other girls I talked to do seem to genuinely like, and learn from, the books. But mostly, the girls play with these dolls the way they play with any dolls. They insert them into various domestic dramas. New school. Orphan. The universally popular "my girl's coming over to your girl's house."
<v Susan Burton>惠特尼的母亲告诉我，惠特尼甚至不喜欢玩偶，直到三年前她得到第一本萨曼莎的书。历史吸引惠特尼喜欢上了这个玩偶。我和许多其他女孩聊过，她们确实似乎真心喜欢并从书中学习。但大多数情况下，女孩们玩这些玩偶的方式和玩任何玩偶一样。她们把玩偶放入各种家庭剧的情境中。新学校、孤儿，还有那个普遍流行的“我的玩偶去你玩偶家做客”。

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:58.250
<v Susan Burton>And they do their hair. I hear that the problem with Molly is that her pigtails always fall out, that most girls prefer to undo Kirsten's braids.
<v Susan Burton>她们还会给玩偶梳头。我听说莫莉的问题是她的辫子总是会散掉，大多数女孩更喜欢解开克里斯汀的辫子。

00:09:58.250 --> 00:10:09.660
<v Whitney Robertson>You get a little piece of the hair, and you brush it from the underside with a certain kind of brush for five minutes. And then it turns out more shiny than it was, and it won't be as frizzy.
<v Whitney Robertson>你取一小撮头发，用一种特定的刷子从下往上刷五分钟。然后头发会比原来更有光泽，也不会那么毛躁了。

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:33.560
<v Susan Burton>The ingenious thing about American Girl Place is not only that they seem to understand what a girl wants to do when she plays with her doll, but that they know how to participate in the game with her. It's as if the marketers have stepped into the center of a girl's dream and are selling to her from a perch in her own unconscious. The doll hair care products in this store rival a beauty salon's offerings. Employees roam the floor, dispensing styling tips.
<v Susan Burton>美国女孩广场的天才之处不仅在于他们似乎了解女孩玩玩偶时想做什么，还在于他们知道如何参与到她的游戏中。就好像营销人员走进了女孩梦境的中心，从她潜意识的制高点向她兜售产品。店里的玩偶美发产品可以媲美美容院的供应。员工在卖场内走动，提供造型技巧。

00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:34.050
<v Whitney Robertson>Frizzy.
<v Whitney Robertson>毛躁。

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:40.530
<v Laura Robertson>The lady was showing them down there how to brush their hair, and how they could put it up into little curlers.
<v Laura Robertson>那位女士在那边演示如何梳理头发，以及如何用小卷发棒把头发卷起来。

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:42.370
<v Susan Burton>Again, Whitney's mother, Laura.
<v Susan Burton>惠特尼的母亲劳拉再次说道。

