<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "AMERICAN GIRL," TOM PETTY AND THE HEARTBREAKERS]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - “美国女孩”，汤姆·佩蒂与伤心人乐队]

00:19:00.540 --> 00:19:23.370
<v Ira Glass>Act Two, You Know What Mr. Bear Would Say. There is a children's book that just came back in print that is truly one of the most remarkable books I have ever seen, the kind of book that all this week, whenever anybody from our staff mentioned the book to somebody who happened to have read it as a child, there was actually this bonding moment. That's the only way to describe it. There would be a bonding moment, and then excited stories about what the book meant to them as a kid.
<v Ira Glass>第二幕，你知道熊先生会说什么。有一本刚重新出版的儿童书，确实是我见过的最了不起的书之一。这种书本周我们员工中无论谁提到它，只要对方小时候读过，真的会产生一种连结时刻。这是描述它的唯一方式。会有一个连结时刻，然后是关于这本书在童年对他们意味着什么的兴奋故事。

00:19:23.370 --> 00:19:29.700
<v Ira Glass>Jean Nathan tells the story of the book and its author, and how the author's life came to resemble something from her book.
<v Ira Glass>简·内森讲述了这本书和它作者的故事，以及作者的生活是如何变得像她书中的东西。

00:19:29.700 --> 00:19:49.620
<v Jean Nathan>It was in the spring of 1997 that the artist's image floated into my mind, the cover of a children's book I hadn't seen, or even thought of, in more than 30 years. There it lodged, and there it remained. It felt as if it were a message, and its insistence startled me. After all, I live among the grown-ups now.
<v Jean Nathan>1997年春天，艺术家的形象浮现在我的脑海中，我三十多年没见过、甚至没想过的一本儿童书的封面。它就在那里扎了根，并一直留在那里。感觉就像一个信息，它的坚持让我吃惊。毕竟，我现在生活在成年人中间。

00:19:49.620 --> 00:20:02.290
<v Jean Nathan>But the image just kept flashing in my mind. Pink and white gingham inset with a black and white photograph of Edith, the lonely doll, an open book between her spread, outstretched legs.
<v Jean Nathan>但这个图像一直在我的脑海中闪现。粉红色和白色格纹背景，镶嵌着一张黑白照片，照片是孤独的玩偶伊迪丝，在她张开的双腿之间摊开一本书。

00:20:02.290 --> 00:20:24.420
<v Jean Nathan>My own copy of The Lonely Doll was long lost. And I soon learned from a bookstore clerk, who gave me the name of the author, Dare Wright, that the book was out of print. The New York Public Library's computer listed three copies, all damaged or missing. When I called a children's book searcher listed in the phone book, she said she knew the book well, and would add my name to her waiting list.
<v Jean Nathan>我自己那本《孤独的玩偶》早已丢失。我很快从书店职员那里得知了作者戴尔·赖特的名字，书已经绝版了。纽约公共图书馆的电脑里有三本，但都有损坏或缺失。当我给电话簿里列出的一位儿童书搜索员打电话时，她说她很了解这本书，并会把我的名字加到她的等候名单上。

00:20:24.420 --> 00:20:50.080
<v Jean Nathan>Meaning to close the phone book, I found myself turning distractedly to the listings for the name Wright. And there, jumping out at me from blurred columns of typeface, was Wright, Dare. 11 East 80th Street, 249-6965. I don't think I could have been any more amazed if the address given had been, say, second to the right, and then straight on till morning, Peter Pan's address on the island of Neverland.
<v Jean Nathan>正打算合上电话簿时，我不经意地翻到了姓“赖特”的列表。就在那里，从模糊的字体列中跳出来的是，赖特，戴尔。东80街11号，249-6965。我想，如果地址是，比如说，向右转再直走直到清晨，彼得·潘在梦幻岛的地址，我也不可能更惊讶了。

00:20:50.080 --> 00:21:15.270
<v Jean Nathan>In the weeks to come, I dialed the number several times. There was never an answer. I also sent a letter expressing how much the book had meant to me as a child, and asking if she knew where I could find a copy. Several months later, I finally received The Lonely Doll from the book searcher.
<v Jean Nathan>在接下来的几周里，我拨了几次这个号码。从来没人接。我还写了一封信，表达了这本书在小时候对我的意义，并询问她是否知道我在哪里能找到一本。几个月后，我终于从图书搜索员那里收到了《孤独的玩偶》。

00:21:15.270 --> 00:21:42.350
<v Jean Nathan>Illustrated with black and white photographs, The Lonely Doll is the story of a doll named Edith, mired in loneliness, seemingly parentless, eating her cereal alone, going to bed alone, begging some pigeons on the windowsill to be her friends, and watching as they just fly away. One morning, two teddy bears, Mr. Bear and Little Bear, appear in her garden. They tell her they've come to be her friends. They move right in.
<v Jean Nathan>《孤独的玩偶》用黑白照片插图，讲述了一个名叫伊迪丝的玩偶的故事，她深陷孤独，似乎没有父母，独自吃麦片，独自上床睡觉，恳求窗台上的鸽子做她的朋友，看着它们飞走。一天早上，两只泰迪熊，熊先生和小熊，出现在她的花园里。它们告诉她它们是来做她朋友的。它们直接搬了进去。

00:21:42.350 --> 00:21:59.970
<v Jean Nathan>Home alone, one rainy day, Edith and Little Bear discover, behind a set of louvered doors, a grown-up woman's closet and dressing room. The identity of the woman, and her absence, are never explained. She might be Edith's mother, but from all indications Edith has no mother.
<v Jean Nathan>一个雨天，独自在家的伊迪丝和小熊发现，在百叶门后面，有一个成年女性的衣橱和更衣室。这个女人的身份和她的缺席从未得到解释。她可能是伊迪丝的母亲，但所有迹象表明伊迪丝没有母亲。

00:21:59.970 --> 00:22:20.070
<v Jean Nathan>A frenzied dress-up session follows, in which Edith and Little Bear adorn themselves with rhinestones and pearls, a petticoat, and a hat with roses and ribbons, high-heeled shoes, a leopard handbag. In their recklessness, they knock over a vase with one long-stemmed rose. The water spills into a jewelry box, but they are oblivious.
<v Jean Nathan>随之而来的是疯狂的换装，伊迪丝和小熊用莱茵石和珍珠、衬裙、带玫瑰和丝带的帽子、高跟鞋、豹纹手袋装饰自己。在鲁莽中，它们打翻了一个插着长茎玫瑰的花瓶。水溅进了首饰盒，但它们全然不知。

00:22:20.070 --> 00:22:40.110
<v Jean Nathan>Wielding a fully swiveled out lipstick, Little Bear goads Edith to put it on. She says she wouldn't dare. You know what Mr. Bear would say. Then, just as Little Bear uses the lipstick to scrawl "Mr. Bear is just a silly old thing" across the oval mirror, who should appear but Mr. Bear.
<v Jean Nathan>小熊拿着一支完全拧出来的口红，怂恿伊迪丝涂上。她说她不敢。你知道熊先生会怎么说。然后，就在小熊用口红在椭圆形的镜子上乱画“熊先生只是个傻老头”时，熊先生出现了。

00:22:40.110 --> 00:23:08.210
<v Jean Nathan>Spankings ensue, but the deeper issue striking terror in Edith is that her disobedience could jeopardize the whole arrangement, and that Edith would be, once again, abandoned and lonely. Yet when the little ones clean up the mess and promise never to do it again, Mr. Bear solemnly promises that he and Little Bear will stay with her forever and ever.
<v Jean Nathan>随之而来的是责打，但让伊迪丝感到恐惧的深层问题是，她的不服从可能会危及整个安排，伊迪丝会再次被遗弃和孤独。然而，当小家伙们清理干净烂摊子并承诺永不再犯时，熊先生庄严地保证他和熊弟弟会永远永远和她在一起。

00:23:08.210 --> 00:23:33.720
<v Jean Nathan>About the time the book arrived, I received a phone call from a woman named Brooke Ashley. She said my letter had been forwarded to her in California. She said she was Dare's unofficial goddaughter and legal guardian, and that Dare, now 84, was in a hospital on life support. She said she was coming to New York to begin closing up Dare's apartment. Did I want to meet her at 11 East 80th Street?
<v Jean Nathan>书到手的那段时间，我接到了一个名叫布鲁克·阿什利的女性的电话。她说我的信被转寄给了她在加州的地址。她说她是戴尔的非官方教女和法定监护人，戴尔现在84岁，在医院依靠生命维持系统。她说她要来纽约开始清理戴尔的公寓。我愿意在东80街11号见她吗？

00:23:33.720 --> 00:23:58.750
<v Jean Nathan>When I arrived at the apartment, Brooke met me at the door and led me to the living room. I was spellbound. The living room was filled with portraits, some life-sized, of a beautiful blond woman. That's Dare, Brooke said. They were all painted by her mother. Dare, I was shocked to realize, looked very much like Edith, the lonely doll. And Edith, I learned, was named for Dare's mother.
<v Jean Nathan>当我到达公寓时，布鲁克在门口迎接我，带我去了客厅。我被迷住了。客厅里挂满了画像，有些是真人大小，是一个美丽的金色头发女人。那是戴尔，布鲁克说。它们都是她母亲画的。我震惊地意识到，戴尔看起来非常像伊迪丝，那个孤独的玩偶。而伊迪丝，我得知，是以戴尔的母亲命名的。

00:23:58.750 --> 00:24:23.330
<v Jean Nathan>As I left, Brooke gave me Dare's fat, leather-bound scrapbook, containing the record of her publishing career, including jacket covers, reviews, and other articles relating to the first 12 of her 19 books for children. The Lonely Doll was her first book. I had never known of the rest.
<v Jean Nathan>我离开时，布鲁克给了我戴尔厚厚的皮面剪贴簿，记录了她的出版生涯，包括书封、评论和其他与她19本童书中的前12本有关的文章。《孤独的玩偶》是她的第一本书。我从不知道其余的。

00:24:23.330 --> 00:24:41.080
<v Jean Nathan>The Lonely Doll received mountains of publicity when it was first published in 1957. It was serialized in Good Housekeeping. It climbed the children's best seller list in the New York Times. It was translated into 6 foreign languages, and remained in print, in various formats, for 35 years.
<v Jean Nathan>《孤独的玩偶》在1957年首次出版时获得了大量的宣传。它在《好管家》杂志上连载。它登上了《纽约时报》儿童畅销书排行榜。它被翻译成6种外语，并以各种形式保持了35年的印刷。

00:24:41.080 --> 00:25:05.920
<v Jean Nathan>I sorted through the piles of clippings and photographs I had been given by Brooke. Mostly, they were photographs of Dare. There were magnificent photos of Dare playing dress-up in elaborate costumes. But the most startling ones depicted Dare across a spectrum of ages in what could be described as undress, or partially so. These photos were just as posed as those of her in costume.
<v Jean Nathan>我整理了布鲁克给我的剪报和照片堆。大部分是戴尔的照片。有戴尔穿着精致服装玩换装的华丽照片。但最令人吃惊的照片描绘了戴尔在不同年龄段处于可被描述为没穿衣服或部分没穿衣服的状态。这些照片和她穿服装的照片一样都是摆拍的。

00:25:05.920 --> 00:25:24.330
<v Jean Nathan>Dare, as I just began to discover, had lived in her own version of a wonderland. I spent the next two years talking to anyone I could find who knew her, trying to make sense of the world I had stumbled into.
<v Jean Nathan>正如我刚开始发现的那样，戴尔生活在属于她自己的仙境版本中。在接下来的两年里，我与任何我能找到认识她的人交谈，试图弄清楚我偶然闯入的世界。

00:25:24.330 --> 00:25:38.980
<v Jean Nathan>In 1917, when Dare was three, her parents divorced. Her father was given custody of Dare's five-year-old brother, Blaine. Her parents seem to have made a pact that the brother and sister should never see each other again.
<v Jean Nathan>1917年，当戴尔三岁时，她的父母离婚了。她的父亲获得了戴尔五岁哥哥布莱恩的监护权。她的父母似乎达成了一个约定，兄妹俩永远不要再见。

00:25:38.980 --> 00:25:57.180
<v Jean Nathan>Dare and her mother moved to Cleveland, Ohio. Her mother put a doll made by the Italian company Lenci on will call at Halle Brothers, a department store near their home, until she could make the full $11.50 payment. The two named the doll Edith.
<v Jean Nathan>戴尔和她的母亲搬到了俄亥俄州克利夫兰。她的母亲在她们家附近的哈勒兄弟百货公司预订了一个由意大利伦奇公司制作的玩偶，直到她能支付全额的11.50美元。两人给那个玩偶取名伊迪丝。

00:25:57.180 --> 00:26:28.450
<v Jean Nathan>Edie, as Dare's mother was known, was a portrait painter of some renown. Those who sat for her included Winston Churchill, Calvin Coolidge, and Greta Garbo. And in 1924, one of her subjects offered her the use of the penthouse of his Cleveland office building as her studio. For the next 45 years, its 1,500 square feet with a northern exposure skylight would be her home. The same year she moved in, Edie enrolled Dare as a boarding student in a private girls' school.
<v Jean Nathan>伊迪丝，也就是戴尔母亲为人所知的名字，是一位享有声誉的肖像画家。她的座上宾包括温斯顿·丘吉尔、卡尔文·柯立芝和葛丽泰·嘉宝。1924年，她的一位模特提议让她使用他位于克利夫兰办公楼顶层作为工作室。在接下来的45年里，那1500平方英尺有着朝北天窗的空间将成为她的家。她搬进去的那一年，伊迪丝让戴尔作为寄宿生进入了一所私立女子学校。

00:26:28.450 --> 00:26:58.560
<v Jean Nathan>Dare graduated from high school in 1934 and moved to New York to attend drama and art school. She didn't know that her brother, Blaine, was also living in the city. Their uncle orchestrated a reunion in Central Park of the 22-year-old tall, handsome Blaine and the 19-year-old newly-golden Dare. According to one of Dare's cousins, Blaine and Dare fell in love. They even considered concealing their brother and sister relationship in order to marry, an idea they eventually dropped.
<v Jean Nathan>戴尔于1934年高中毕业，搬到纽约参加戏剧和艺术学校。她不知道她的哥哥布莱恩也住在城里。他们的叔叔在中央公园安排了一次团聚，对象是22岁高大英俊的布莱恩和19岁刚刚变成金发女郎的戴尔。根据戴尔的一位表亲，布莱恩和戴尔坠入爱河。他们甚至考虑隐瞒兄妹关系以便结婚，后来他们放弃了这个想法。

00:26:58.560 --> 00:27:18.830
<v Jean Nathan>Dare tried, with little success, to be an actress, and then went on to work as a fashion model, including a stint as the Maidenform bra girl. Soon she switched to the other side of the camera, and her fashion photography began appearing in Vogue, Harper's Bazaar, and Town and Country.
<v Jean Nathan>戴尔尝试成为一名演员，但不太成功，后来做了一名时装模特，包括在Maidenform内衣公司做过一阵模特。很快她转到摄像机另一侧，她的时尚摄影作品开始出现在《时尚》、《时尚芭莎》和《城与乡》杂志上。

00:27:18.830 --> 00:27:36.470
<v Jean Nathan>In the early '40s, Blaine introduced Dare to a British friend of his, named Philip Sandeman, whose family produced port and sherry. Though tall, blond, and dazzlingly beautiful, Dare was strangely childlike, avoidant, and shy, and seemed somehow confused by her powers over men.
<v Jean Nathan>40年代初，布莱恩向戴尔介绍了他的英国朋友菲利普·桑德曼，他的家族生产波特酒和雪利酒。虽然高挑、金发且美得令人目眩，戴尔却有一种奇怪的孩童气、回避且害羞，似乎对她对男人的吸引力感到困惑。

00:27:36.470 --> 00:27:50.940
<v Jean Nathan>Philip was ideal for Dare. Throughout their five year courtship, he remained at a safe distance. First there was the war, and afterward he lived across the ocean. They were engaged, but a week before the wedding, Philip called things off.
<v Jean Nathan>菲利普是戴尔的理想人选。在他们五年的求爱过程中，他一直保持着安全的距离。首先是战争，之后他住在海洋彼岸。他们订了婚，但在婚礼前一周，菲利普取消了婚约。

00:27:50.940 --> 00:28:07.470
<v Jean Nathan>In the years that followed, Dare's close friend, Donald Sewell, said, men always did beat a path to her door. Dare wasn't interested. She never seemed quite of this world, he said. She was ethereal, somehow above normal courtship.
<v Jean Nathan>在接下来的几年里，戴尔的好友唐纳德·休厄尔说，男人们总是踏破她的门槛。戴尔对此不感兴趣。她说，她似乎从来不完全属于这个世界。她是超凡脱俗的，不知何故凌驾于正常的求爱之上。

00:28:07.470 --> 00:28:33.390
<v Jean Nathan>Throughout all of this, Dare seems to have hung onto the dream that Philip would come back to her. Edie no doubt fanned this fantasy. Dare sent Philip's mother photographs of alluring portraits Edie painted of her in this period. In all of them, Dare had posed in low-cut white gowns. Edie also did many portraits of Philip, painting him from Dare's photographs. These were signed by both mother and daughter.
<v Jean Nathan>在此期间，戴尔似乎一直坚持着菲利普会回到她身边的梦想。伊迪丝无疑煽动了这个幻想。戴尔给菲利普的母亲寄去了她在此期间拍摄的迷人肖像画照片，照片上全是伊迪丝画的。在所有这些画中，戴尔都穿着低胸的白色礼服。伊迪丝也画了许多菲利普的肖像，根据戴尔的照片绘画。这些画作由母亲和女儿共同签名。

00:28:33.390 --> 00:29:02.570
<v Jean Nathan>Dare also staged elaborate photographs of herself, seated at her dressing table, a sort of shrine to Philip, with a pencil drawing of him that she had sketched, and a bottle of Sandeman sherry. Clutched in her hands is a letter he had sent to her. She is turned, glancing at the camera wistfully, hopefully. But in 1951, at the age of 30, Philip was killed flying in a demonstration airshow.
<v Jean Nathan>戴尔还精心策划了她自己的照片，坐在梳妆台前，某种程度上是菲利普的神龛，放着一张她画的他的铅笔素描，和一瓶桑德曼雪利酒。手里紧紧抓着一封他寄给她的信。她转过身，忧郁而充满希望地看着镜头。但在1951年，30岁那年，菲利普死于一场飞行表演。

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:32.560
<v Jean Nathan>After Philip's death, Dare rented her first New York apartment, having lived until then in a series of hotel rooms. She hung her portraits of Philip throughout the apartment, with one by the front door. She made voluminous drapes for the windows and installed smoked glass mirrors. Her all-white boudoir was fitted out with a taffeta canopied princess bed. And next to that, set off by louvered doors, was a dressing room with a vanity table, an oval mirror, and a large closet.
<v Jean Nathan>菲利普去世后，戴尔租了她的第一间纽约公寓，此前她一直住在旅馆里。她把菲利普的肖像挂在公寓各处，门口就有一幅。她为窗户做了宽大的帷幔，安装了烟熏玻璃镜。她全白色的闺房配有一张带塔夫绸帷幔的公主床。旁边，由百叶门隔开，是一间更衣室，里面有梳妆台、椭圆形镜子和一个大衣橱。

00:29:32.560 --> 00:30:03.280
<v Jean Nathan>Dare and her mother were virtually inseparable. Dare made frequent visits to her mother's penthouse in Cleveland. And Edie spent at least one week a month in New York with Dare, sleeping, as was their custom, in the same bed. They seemed to have no idea of the oddness of this arrangement. As Edie told Brooke, I reach over and pat her little bottom in the night.
<v Jean Nathan>戴尔和她母亲几乎形影不离。戴尔经常去她在克利夫兰的母亲的顶层公寓。伊迪丝每个月至少在纽约和戴尔待一周，按照她们的习惯，睡在同一张床上。她们似乎对这种安排的奇特毫无察觉。正如伊迪丝告诉布鲁克的，我在夜里伸手拍拍她的小屁股。

00:30:03.280 --> 00:30:29.110
<v Jean Nathan>Now that Dare had her own apartment, Edie sent along her belongings, including her childhood dolls. After the first, and biggest, trauma of Dare's life, losing her father and brother, consolation had come in the form of Edith, her doll. Now, after the second, losing Philip, Edith was back again.
<v Jean Nathan>现在戴尔有了自己的公寓，伊迪丝送来了她的个人物品，包括她童年的玩偶。在戴尔人生中第一次也是最大的创伤——失去父亲和哥哥——之后，慰藉以伊迪丝，她的玩偶的形式到来。现在，在第二次——失去菲利普——之后，伊迪丝又回来了。

00:30:29.110 --> 00:30:50.020
<v Jean Nathan>Edith was a mess. Her wig was yellowed and tangled, and her clothes were in shreds. Dare quickly set about giving her a completely new look. Long, straight blond hair, usually worn tied up in a high ponytail, and gold hoop earrings. Edith the doll now looked very like Dare herself.
<v Jean Nathan>伊迪丝一团糟。她的假发发黄打结，衣服撕成了碎片。戴尔很快着手给她换了一个全新的造型。长而直的金发，通常扎成高高的马尾，戴着金色的圆圈耳环。伊迪丝玩偶现在看起来非常像戴尔本人。

00:30:50.020 --> 00:31:15.240
<v Jean Nathan>I don't think of Edith as a doll, Dare told an interviewer. She's a personality in her own right. A suitor told me that one day it began with Dare chiding, you didn't say hello to Edith, as though he had rudely neglected to acknowledge a human presence. Another time, when he tried to kiss her, she held the doll out in front of her to block his advances.
<v Jean Nathan>我没把伊迪丝看作一个玩偶，戴尔告诉一位采访者。她本身就是一个个性。一位追求者告诉我，有一天戴尔指责他，你没和伊迪丝打招呼，好像他粗鲁地忽略了一个人类的存在。还有一次，当他试图吻她时，她把玩偶举在前面挡住了他的亲昵。

00:31:15.240 --> 00:31:38.970
<v Jean Nathan>Dare also acquired some teddy bears in this period, after her brother went on a drunken shopping spree with Dorothy Tivis Pollack, a former model, who headed the Figureheads modeling agency where Dare was registered for a time. Blaine was drunk, Dorothy told me, and got weird, as he always did when he drank. We passed FAO Schwarz, and he saw a teddy bear in the window. He decided I had to have one. In we went.
<v Jean Nathan>在此期间，戴尔也得到了一些泰迪熊，那是她的哥哥和前模特、曾经负责戴尔注册的Figureheads模特经纪公司的多萝西·提维斯·波拉克进行了一场醉酒购物狂欢后。多萝西告诉我，布莱恩当时喝醉了，变得很古怪，他喝酒时总是这样。我们经过FAO Schwarz，他看到橱窗里有一只泰迪熊。他决定我必须有一只。我们进去了。

00:31:38.970 --> 00:31:58.230
<v Jean Nathan>But when he saw the bears, he said it would be terrible to buy just one, because the bear would be lonely. With that, he directed the saleswoman to pack up the entire lot, about a dozen stuffed bears. Hundreds of dollars of bears. Since Dare's apartment was just around the corner, we went over there, carrying all these damn teddy bears.
<v Jean Nathan>但当他看到这些熊时，他说只买一只太糟糕了，因为那只熊会感到孤独。于是，他指示售货员把整批，大约十几只毛绒熊都包起来。价值几百美元的熊。因为戴尔的公寓就在拐角处，我们带着所有这些该死的泰迪熊去了那里。

00:31:58.230 --> 00:32:12.010
<v Jean Nathan>Within minutes of their arrival, Dorothy recalled, Blaine and Dare were seated on the floor, surrounded by bears, telling bear stories in various bear voices. Soon Dare added Edith, the doll, to the party.
<v Jean Nathan>到达后几分钟，多萝西回忆道，布莱恩和戴尔坐在地板上，被熊群包围着，用各种熊的声音讲熊的故事。很快戴尔把伊迪丝，那个玩偶，也加到了聚会中。

00:32:12.010 --> 00:32:42.340
<v Jean Nathan>Edith, Dare later told an interviewer, looked so happy with the bears that she decided to photograph them all together. Soon, she was making them into a book. She thought up a storyline, made Edith outfits, and photographed her doll and bears in her apartment and around New York. In all her poses, Edith appears to be an animated, thinking, feeling little girl.
<v Jean Nathan>戴尔后来告诉采访者，伊迪丝和熊在一起看起来很开心，所以她决定把它们聚在一起拍照。很快，她就把它们做成了一本书。她想出了一个故事情节，给伊迪丝做了衣服，并在她的公寓和纽约各地拍摄她的玩偶和熊。在所有的摆拍中，伊迪丝看起来就像一个生动、有思想、有感情的小女孩。

00:32:42.340 --> 00:33:15.700
<v Jean Nathan>The story of the lonely doll was, in some measure, Dare's own story. The book and her life share the same set of themes. Seeking love and approval, fearing abandonment, risking separation and autonomy. In other words, the issues inherent in growing up. But in the book, she found a way to make it right, removing a mother from the proceedings, and providing her alter ego, Edith, with love and rescue in the form of two mail teddy bears.
<v Jean Nathan>《孤独的玩偶》的故事在某种程度上也是戴尔自己的故事。这本书和她的生活有着相同的主题。寻求爱和认可，害怕被遗弃，冒险分离和自主。换句话说，成长中固有的问题。但在书中，她找到了一种让事情变好的方法，从情节中移除了一位母亲，并以两只邮来的泰迪熊的形式为她的化身伊迪丝提供了爱和拯救。

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:34.820
<v Jean Nathan>With the publication of The Lonely Doll, and the 18 books that followed, came recognition that Dare had never experienced, and with which she seemed highly uncomfortable. She would have been 43 in 1957, the year the book was published. 48 when she gave her age as 35 to the Saturday Evening Post.
<v Jean Nathan>随着《孤独的玩偶》及其随后18本书的出版，带来了戴尔从未经历过的认可，而她似乎对这种认可感到极度不适。1957年，即该书出版那年，她本该43岁。当她向《周六晚邮报》谎报年龄为35岁时，她已经48岁了。

00:33:34.820 --> 00:38:12.600
<v Jean Nathan>Her mother, Edie, however, was completely at home with all the attention. The two became a sort of team, with Edie in the assistant's role. They adored traveling, and built it into the story lines of Dare's books, the most elaborate one being Lona: A Fairy Tale. For this, they traveled throughout Europe, scouting out scenery for the story of a princess who must overcome the spell of an evil wizard who has changed her into a tiny doll. Dare herself posed as the princess in her full-size incarnation. She would set up the shot, focus the camera, don her gown, and have her mother trip the shutter.
<v Jean Nathan>然而，她的母亲伊迪丝对所有的关注感到完全自在。两人成了某种团队，伊迪丝担任助理角色。她们热爱旅行，并将其融入戴尔书中的情节，最复杂的一本是《洛娜：一个童话故事》。为此，她们游遍了欧洲，为这个故事 scouting 场景，讲述了一位公主必须克服一个邪恶巫师的咒语，因为他把她变成了一个微小的玩偶。戴尔本人在她的全尺寸化身中扮演公主。她会设置拍摄点，对焦相机，穿上礼服，让她的母亲按下快门。

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:23.110
<v Ira Glass>A version of Jean Nathan's story first appeared in the literary journal Tin House. She's working on a book about Dare Wright to be published by Random House.
<v Ira Glass>简·内森文章的一个版本首次出现在文学杂志《锡屋》上。她正在写一本关于戴尔·赖特的书，将由兰登书屋出版。

00:38:23.110 --> 00:39:34.480
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, girl turns helicopter into baby and other true stories, in a minute, from Public Radio International, when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，女孩把直升机变成婴儿以及其他真实故事，一分钟后，来自国际公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:39:34.480 --> 00:39:43.020
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life . I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme, bring you a variety of different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's program, dolls.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们选择一个主题，为您带来关于该主题的各种不同类型的故事。今天的节目，玩偶。

00:39:43.020 --> 00:40:03.950
<v Vivian Paley>Most important, from the beginning, they are characters to be nurtured. Hugging it, feeling it against your cheek, rocking it, just having it. It's a bulwark against loneliness.
<v Vivian Paley>最重要的是，从一开始，它们就是需要被培育的角色。拥抱它，感受它贴着你的脸颊，摇晃它，仅仅拥有它。这是对抗孤独的堡垒。

00:40:03.950 --> 00:40:30.580
<v Ira Glass>Vivian Paley was a kindergarten teacher for decades, won the MacArthur Genius Grant for her many books about the stories that children invent, and the way they play, and what it's about. She says, children use dolls and they make up stories for similar reason, to act out moments and feelings that are knocking around in their heads. In her classroom, Vivian Paley would have her students invent stories and then act them out. And she says, there's one idea that comes up in small children's stories more than any other.
<v Ira Glass>薇薇安·佩利做了几十年的幼儿园老师，因其关于孩子们发明的各种故事及其玩耍方式以及这其中意义的书籍而获得了麦克阿瑟天才奖。她说，孩子们使用玩偶并编造故事是因为类似的原因，为了演绎那些在他们脑海中碰撞的时刻和感受。在她的课堂上，薇薇安·佩利会让学生们发明故事并演出来。她说，有一个想法在幼儿故事中出现的频率比任何其他想法都高。

00:40:30.580 --> 00:41:08.580
<v Vivian Paley>So many of the stories that young children tell have to do with an animal or a little girl or boy being lonely, and walking in the woods, and finding someone to play with. It is the universal, most successful story. And if you have a kindergarten class, or a preschool class, or a first grade class, and-- although this would never happen-- but let's say, all 24 children told this story.
<v Vivian Paley>幼儿讲述的许多故事都与一只动物或一个小女孩或小男孩感到孤独，在森林里走，并找到可以一起玩的人有关。这是普遍的、最成功的故事。如果你有一个幼儿园班级，或者一个学前班，或者一年级班级，并且——虽然这种情况永远不会发生——但假设，所有24个孩子都讲了这个故事。

00:41:08.580 --> 00:41:22.020
<v Vivian Paley>There was a lonely deer. He had no one to play with. And then a cougar came up and said, I'll be your friend. Next story. There was a lonely boy. He had no one to play with. And on and on.
<v Vivian Paley>有一只孤独的鹿。他没有人和他一起玩。然后一只美洲狮走过来说，我会做你的朋友。下一个故事。有一个孤独的男孩。他没有人和他一起玩。如此循环往复。

00:41:22.020 --> 00:41:44.160
<v Vivian Paley>Not one child in the class would say, what, again? Haven't we had enough of that already? No. They would say, more. More. Tell us again that when you're lonely, someone's going to come up and say, I'll play with you.
<v Vivian Paley>班上没有一个孩子会说，什么，又是这个？我们还没听够吗？不。他们会说，更多。更多。再告诉我们一次，当你孤独时，会有人过来对你说，我会和你一起玩。

00:41:44.160 --> 00:42:01.550
<v Ira Glass>Vivian Paley says that it's by making up stories together that small children become friends. In one of her books, she tells the story of one of the children in her classroom who used a doll and a story to isolate himself from other students, and how it was through other stories and dolls that other children finally pulled him out of his isolation.
<v Ira Glass>薇薇安·佩利说，正是通过一起编造故事，幼儿才能成为朋友。在她的一本书中，她讲述了班上的一个孩子如何使用玩偶和一个故事将自己与同学隔离开来，以及正是通过其他故事和玩偶，其他孩子最终将他从孤立中拉了出来。

00:42:01.550 --> 00:42:14.240
<v Ira Glass>The doll in this case was a toy helicopter. Vivian now tells the story with actors recreating scenes from her classroom from transcriptions of what really occurred.
<v Ira Glass>在这个例子中，玩偶是一个玩具直升机。薇薇安现在用演员重现她课堂上的场景，这些场景来自实际发生情况的记录。

00:42:14.240 --> 00:42:32.140
<v Vivian Paley>Every day in the corner of the classroom, nestled between the black cabinet and the bay window bench, Jason arranges big wooden blocks into walls and crouches behind them. He calls this his airport. The walls surround him, high enough so he can barely be seen over the top.
<v Vivian Paley>每天在教室的角落里，依偎在黑色柜子和飘窗长椅之间，杰森把大木块排列成墙，蹲在墙后面。他称这为他的机场。墙壁包围着他，高到他几乎从上面看不见。

00:42:32.140 --> 00:42:49.490
<v Jason>This blade is turning around. Now you're going faster. Now you're going faster. Now you're going to crash. Now you're going off the ground. And now you're going up, up, up. Now you're going loud. Brrrrm. Now you're going to land. OK. All safely.
<v Jason>这个桨叶在转动。现在你转得更快了。现在你转得更快了。现在你要坠毁了。现在你要离开地面了。现在你要上升，上升，上升了。现在你要发出巨大的声音了。呜呜呜。现在你要着陆了。好了。一切安全。

00:42:49.490 --> 00:43:03.050
<v Vivian Paley>Jason plays alone. He tells stories to himself. He seems unaware of our habits and customs. He wails in fright if his helicopter is touched, and he breaks up our talk with ear-splitting noises.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森独自玩耍。他对自己讲故事。他似乎不知道我们的习惯和习俗。如果他的直升机被触碰，他会发出恐惧的哀号，并用刺耳的噪音打破我们的谈话。

00:43:03.050 --> 00:43:08.740
<v Jason>[MAKING NOISES]
<v Jason>[发出噪音]

00:43:08.740 --> 00:43:26.000
<v Vivian Paley>His helicopter story isolates him. If you ask him a question, he says his helicopter is broken. If you suggest an activity, he rushes away to fix his helicopter. He hasn't learned to listen to anyone else's story, and stories are the way children get to know each other.
<v Vivian Paley>他的直升机故事使他与世隔绝。如果你问他一个问题，他说他的直升机坏了。如果你建议一项活动，他会冲去修理他的直升机。他还没学会倾听别人的故事，而故事是孩子们互相了解的方式。

00:43:26.000 --> 00:43:31.440
<v Jason>[MAKING NOISES]
<v Jason>[发出噪音]

00:43:31.440 --> 00:43:34.690
<v Joseph>Pretend a bad alligator peeked downstairs.
<v Joseph>假装一只坏鳄鱼偷看了楼下。

00:43:34.690 --> 00:43:37.910
<v Simon>Yeah, and then we hiss him. Then we fighted him.
<v Simon>是啊，然后我们嘘他。然后我们和他打了一架。

00:43:37.910 --> 00:43:43.380
<v Joseph>But who's the alligator? Hey, Jason. You're the alligator. OK, Jason?
<v Joseph>但谁是鳄鱼？嘿，杰森。你是鳄鱼。好吗，杰森？

00:43:43.380 --> 00:43:49.610
<v Jason>Up, up, up. Down, down, down. Oh, we're going to crash.
<v Jason>上升，上升，上升。下降，下降，下降。哦，我们要坠毁了。

00:43:49.610 --> 00:44:07.760
<v Vivian Paley>Children usually take part in each other's stories because they need friends and they want to be part of a drama. If Jason would agree to be the alligator, Joseph and Simon would later tell their families, Jason is my friend. It's that easy when it works.
<v Vivian Paley>孩子们通常会参与彼此的故事，因为他们需要朋友，想要成为戏剧的一部分。如果杰森同意做鳄鱼，约瑟夫和西蒙以后会告诉他们的家人，杰森是我的朋友。当它起作用时，一切就是这么简单。

00:44:07.760 --> 00:44:11.300
<v Simon>Jason, look at me. You want to be an alligator or not?
<v Simon>杰森，看着我。你想做鳄鱼还是不想？

00:44:11.300 --> 00:44:16.500
<v Jason>This is a rescue helicopter. Someone broke it. I have to fix the blades.
<v Jason>这是一架救援直升机。有人弄坏了它。我必须修理桨叶。

00:44:16.500 --> 00:44:28.380
<v Joseph>I got an idea, Jason. Your helicopter has to rescue us, OK? Help, help. A monster alligator in the dark. Save me, helicopter. To the rescue. Save the day.
<v Joseph>我有办法了，杰森。你的直升机必须救援我们，好吗？救命，救命。黑暗中有一只怪兽鳄鱼。救救我，直升机。来救援吧。拯救这一天。

00:44:28.380 --> 00:44:36.770
<v Vivian Paley>Jason is silent, bent over his helicopter. Joseph glares at him. His best logic isn't working, and he's puzzled.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森沉默了，弯腰盯着他的直升机。约瑟夫瞪着他。他最好的逻辑不起作用，他感到困惑。

00:44:36.770 --> 00:44:41.960
<v Vivian Paley>I heard Joseph call for help, Jason. Can your helicopter save the snakes?
<v Vivian Paley>我听见约瑟夫呼救了，杰森。你的直升机能救那些蛇吗？

00:44:41.960 --> 00:44:45.600
<v Jason>My blade is broken. Someone broke this blade.
<v Jason>我的桨叶坏了。有人弄坏了这个桨叶。

00:44:45.600 --> 00:44:48.290
<v Vivian Paley>Can you show Joseph and Simon how you fix it?
<v Vivian Paley>你能给约瑟夫和西蒙展示一下你是怎么修理它的吗？

00:44:48.290 --> 00:44:51.320
<v Jason>I can't show someone how you fix it.
<v Jason>我不能给别人看你是怎么修理它的。

00:44:51.320 --> 00:45:13.350
<v Vivian Paley>He calls the children someone. After two months, he refers to no one but me by name. He enters the classroom feeling different, frightened, attacked, that he must defend himself. But every child knows these feelings, in one way or another.
<v Vivian Paley>他称孩子们为“别人”。两个月后，除了我，他指代任何人都不称呼名字。他走进教室时感到与众不同，感到害怕，感到被攻击，他必须保护自己。但每个孩子以这样或那样的方式都知道这些感觉。

00:45:13.350 --> 00:45:37.040
<v Vivian Paley>Every day, after I've collected 10 or 12 stories, we act the stories out. I read each story out loud, and the children take different roles. The stage where we do this is a taped square in the center of the story room rug. It is sacrosanct when stories are performed. The children learn to keep off the stage unless they are in the story.
<v Vivian Paley>每天，在我收集了10到12个故事后，我们把故事演出来。我大声读出每个故事，孩子们扮演不同的角色。我们进行表演的舞台是故事室地毯中央的一个用胶带粘出的方块。当故事被表演时，这里是神圣不可侵犯的。孩子们学会了除非是在故事中，否则不要踏上舞台。

00:45:37.040 --> 00:45:50.060
<v Vivian Paley>Jason refuses to follow the rule, and it upsets every one. His motor tunes up as each story begins, and within a sentence or two he is flying around the stage. He does this as we act out Simon's story.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森拒绝遵守规则，这让每个人都很不安。随着每个故事的开始，他的引擎就开始轰鸣，一两句话之内，他就在舞台上飞来飞去了。当我们在表演西蒙的故事时，他做了同样的事情。

00:45:50.060 --> 00:45:54.670
<v Jason>[MAKING  NOISES]
<v Jason>[发出噪音]

00:45:54.670 --> 00:45:57.460
<v Vivian Paley>Simon, is there a helicopter in this story?
<v Vivian Paley>西蒙，这个故事里有直升机吗？

00:45:57.460 --> 00:45:58.120
<v Simon>No.
<v Simon>没有。

00:45:58.120 --> 00:46:01.200
<v Vivian Paley>Then you mustn't come on stage, Jason.
<v Vivian Paley>那么你不可以上台，杰森。

00:46:01.200 --> 00:46:13.240
<v Ira Glass>Jason has heard this reasoning before. I ask this question all the time when he forges on stage. Is there a helicopter in this story? The answer is always no.
<v Ira Glass>杰森以前听过这种推理。每当他冲上舞台时，我总是问这个问题。这个故事里有直升机吗？答案总是没有。

00:46:13.240 --> 00:46:17.880
<v Vivian Paley>Simon, is there a helicopter in your story? Do the squirrels see a helicopter?
<v Vivian Paley>西蒙，你的故事里有直升机吗？松鼠们看到直升机了吗？

00:46:17.880 --> 00:46:27.490
<v Simon>No. Yeah. They do. They heard it flying over there, then it lands, on this spot, right here.
<v Simon>没有。啊。它们看到了。它们听见它在那边飞，然后它着陆了，就在这个位置，就在这儿。

00:46:27.490 --> 00:46:31.900
<v Jason>[MAKING NOISES] I turn off the motor.
<v Jason>[发出噪音] 我关掉了引擎。

00:46:31.900 --> 00:46:44.220
<v Vivian Paley>Jason stops, just where Simon pointed. He has deliberately furthered another child's story. Today, Jason has listened.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森停了下来，就在西蒙指的地方。他特意推动了另一个孩子的故事。今天，杰森听进去了。

00:46:44.220 --> 00:46:57.830
<v Vivian Paley>The next day, I tell Jason, you turned off your motor when Simon told you to land. Now you know all about stories. Do you want to tell one? He says yes, and launches right in.
<v Vivian Paley>第二天，我对杰森说，当西蒙让你着陆时，你关掉了你的引擎。现在你很了解故事了。你想讲一个吗？他说好，并且直接开始了。

00:46:57.830 --> 00:47:03.320
<v Jason>And a helicopter, a turbo prop. It's flying.
<v Jason>还有一架直升机，一个涡轮螺旋桨。它在飞。

00:47:03.320 --> 00:47:18.060
<v Vivian Paley>How could his first story be anything else? In the story room, Jason zooms around the rug as I read his story. He continues to fly several moments after I'm done reading.
<v Vivian Paley>他的第一个故事怎么可能是别的呢？在故事室里，当我读他的故事时，杰森在地毯周围嗖嗖地飞。在我读完后，他继续飞了几分钟。

00:47:18.060 --> 00:47:21.020
<v Vivian Paley>I wonder if the helicopter sees another plane?
<v Vivian Paley>我想知道直升机有没有看到另一架飞机？

00:47:21.020 --> 00:47:22.220
<v Jason>Someone.
<v Jason>别人。

00:47:22.220 --> 00:47:23.250
<v Vivian Paley>Which someone?
<v Vivian Paley>哪个别人？

00:47:23.250 --> 00:47:24.520
<v Jason>Squirrel someone.
<v Jason>松鼠别人。

00:47:24.520 --> 00:47:28.050
<v Simon>He means me. I'm the plane, right?
<v Simon>他是指我。我是飞机，对吧？

00:47:28.050 --> 00:47:45.410
<v Vivian Paley>Jason nods, and the two aircraft zoom around the room. Chins forward, arms in motion, the two boys fly together in formation.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森点了点头，两架飞机在房间周围嗖嗖地飞。下巴向前，手臂在运动，两个男孩结队飞行。

00:47:45.410 --> 00:48:08.520
<v Vivian Paley>Young children see themselves always inside a story. A young child will spend easily 3/4 of his time in fantasy play. It's through stories and fantasies that children understand the world. It is their intuitive approach to all occasions. It is the way they think.
<v Vivian Paley>幼儿总是把自己看作故事的一部分。幼儿很容易花3/4的时间进行幻想游戏。正是通过故事和幻想，孩子们了解世界。这是他们对所有场合的直觉方法。这就是他们的思考方式。

00:48:08.520 --> 00:48:10.310
<v Vivian Paley>Jason still plays alone.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森仍然独自玩耍。

00:48:10.310 --> 00:48:15.030
<v Jason>I want to. I always have to be a helicopter. But you always tell me not to.
<v Jason>我想这样做。我总是必须是一架直升机。但你总是告诉我不要。

00:48:15.030 --> 00:48:23.410
<v Vivian Paley>I didn't tell you not to be a helicopter, Jason. But Simon asked you to come into his squirrelly hole. And I thought you didn't hear him since you didn't answer.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森，我没让你不要做直升机。但西蒙请你进入他的松鼠洞。我想你没听见他，因为你没回答。

00:48:23.410 --> 00:48:26.300
<v Jason>Because that squirrelly guy is outside the window.
<v Jason>因为那个松鼠家伙在窗外。

00:48:26.300 --> 00:48:29.960
<v Simon>I'm not outside. Don't say that.
<v Simon>我不在外面。别那样说。

00:48:29.960 --> 00:48:33.850
<v Vivian Paley>Jason seems surprised that he's gotten such an intense response.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森似乎对自己得到了如此强烈的回应感到惊讶。

00:48:33.850 --> 00:48:36.530
<v Jason>That squirrel someone is outside.
<v Jason>那个松鼠别人在外面。

00:48:36.530 --> 00:48:47.630
<v Simon>I hate you, Jason. You're never my friend.
<v Simon>我讨厌你，杰森。你永远不是我的朋友。

00:48:47.630 --> 00:48:53.040
<v Vivian Paley>Jason still doesn't play with the other children. But one day, he points at Simon.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森仍然不和其他孩子玩。但有一天，他指着西蒙。

00:48:53.040 --> 00:48:55.130
<v Jason>Someone's hiding in my airport.
<v Jason>有人藏在我的机场里。

00:48:55.130 --> 00:48:57.370
<v Simon>No. I'm not in there.
<v Simon>不。我没在里面。

00:48:57.370 --> 00:48:59.320
<v Jason>Simon is in my airport.
<v Jason>西蒙在我的机场里。

00:48:59.320 --> 00:49:01.180
<v Simon>You're lying, you doody head.
<v Simon>你在撒谎，你个笨蛋。

00:49:01.180 --> 00:49:03.210
<v Jason>Simon is in my airport.
<v Jason>西蒙在我的机场里。

00:49:03.210 --> 00:49:06.360
<v Simon>Don't say my name. Tell him not to, teacher.
<v Simon>别叫我的名字。告诉他不要，老师。

00:49:06.360 --> 00:49:13.760
<v Vivian Paley>Jason, Simon doesn't want you to pretend he's in your airport. Do you really want him to come in? Really come in?
<v Vivian Paley>杰森，西蒙不希望你假装他在你的机场里。你真的希望他进来吗？真的进来吗？

00:49:13.760 --> 00:49:15.600
<v Jason>No.
<v Jason>不。

00:49:15.600 --> 00:49:24.760
<v Vivian Paley>Jason, can I help you find someone to play inside your airport? Teachers do that, you know. Once I helped Simon find a mother squirrel. Remember, Simon?
<v Vivian Paley>杰森，我能帮你找个人在你的机场里玩吗？老师会做那种事，你知道的。曾经我帮西蒙找过一只松鼠妈妈。记得吗，西蒙？

00:49:24.760 --> 00:49:26.990
<v Jason>I don't want a mother squirrel.
<v Jason>我不要松鼠妈妈。

00:49:26.990 --> 00:49:28.430
<v Vivian Paley>Who do you want?
<v Vivian Paley>你想要谁？

00:49:28.430 --> 00:49:31.230
<v Jason>Someone hiding in my airport.
<v Jason>有人藏在我的机场里。

00:49:31.230 --> 00:49:36.990
<v Vivian Paley>OK. Listen everyone. Jason needs someone to hide in his airport. Who will do that?
<v Vivian Paley>好的。听着大家。杰森需要有人藏在他的机场里。谁愿意那样做？

00:49:36.990 --> 00:49:39.340
<v Samantha>Only if I can be the mother.
<v Samantha>除非我能当妈妈。

00:49:39.340 --> 00:49:41.690
<v Jason>It's a helicopter house.
<v Jason>这是一个直升机房子。

00:49:41.690 --> 00:49:44.010
<v Samantha>I'll be the mother, and you be the baby.
<v Samantha>我当妈妈，你当宝宝。

00:49:44.010 --> 00:49:45.090
<v Jason>No.
<v Jason>不。

00:49:45.090 --> 00:49:47.820
<v Samantha>I'm She-Ra and you're the helicopter?
<v Samantha>我是希瑞，你是直升机？

00:49:47.820 --> 00:49:49.000
<v Jason>Yes.
<v Jason>是的。

00:49:49.000 --> 00:49:54.880
<v Vivian Paley>Samantha climbs behind the wall of blocks gingerly, and sits down next to Jason.
<v Vivian Paley>萨曼莎小心翼翼地爬到积木墙后，坐在杰森旁边。

00:49:54.880 --> 00:49:57.670
<v Jason>My blades are broken. I'm fixing them.
<v Jason>我的桨叶坏了。我在修理它们。

00:49:57.670 --> 00:50:02.280
<v Samantha>We have to make beds. I'll get the pillows. Save my place.
<v Samantha>我们必须铺床。我去拿枕头。给我留个位置。

00:50:02.280 --> 00:50:07.850
<v Vivian Paley>Jason covers Samantha's place with one hand, and blows on the helicopter blades.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森用一只手盖住萨曼莎的位置，并对着直升机桨叶吹气。

00:50:07.850 --> 00:50:17.990
<v Jason>[BLOWS] Turn around. Turn around. Ooh, not such a good spinning. Kick the house down. Kick the house down.
<v Jason>[吹气] 转动。转动。噢，旋转得不太好。把房子踢倒。把房子踢倒。

00:50:17.990 --> 00:50:21.260
<v Samantha>Lie down, little helicopter.
<v Samantha>躺下，小直升机。

00:50:21.260 --> 00:50:28.160
<v Vivian Paley>She has pillows and sheets, and she makes Jason into a baby as delicately as she can.
<v Vivian Paley>她有枕头和被子，她尽可能细腻地把杰森变成了一个婴儿。

00:50:28.160 --> 00:50:31.020
<v Jason>Kick the house down.
<v Jason>把房子踢倒。

00:50:31.020 --> 00:50:34.360
<v Samantha>Shh, little helicopter.
<v Samantha>嘘，小直升机。

00:50:34.360 --> 00:50:56.650
<v Vivian Paley>With this one episode, Samantha launches a wholehearted pursuit of Jason that, more than any other event in the school year, brings him out of the helicopter house, and into the social life of the classroom. She is determined to make Jason into her baby, however he may object.
<v Vivian Paley>通过这一个小插曲，萨曼莎启动了对杰森的全心全意的追求，这比学年中任何其他事件都更能让他走出直升机房子，进入教室的社交生活。她决心要把杰森变成她的婴儿，无论他如何反抗。

00:50:56.650 --> 00:51:09.250
<v Vivian Paley>Other times, Jason shows he's unable to join the other children's fantasy play. When he wanders into a game the boys are playing, he doesn't understand what they're doing, and everyone yells at him to get out.
<v Vivian Paley>其他时候，杰森显示出他无法加入其他孩子的幻想游戏。当他漫无目的地走进男孩们正在玩的游戏时，他不明白他们在做什么，大家都会大声叫他滚开。

00:51:09.250 --> 00:51:13.660
<v Joseph>Don't go there, Jason. You're breaking up the marshway.
<v Joseph>别去那儿，杰森。你打断了沼泽路。

00:51:13.660 --> 00:51:15.620
<v Alex>You're breaking up the marshway.
<v Alex>你打断了沼泽路。

00:51:15.620 --> 00:51:17.580
<v Joseph>Get out, Jason.
<v Joseph>滚开，杰森。

00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:19.050
<v Alex>Don't. It's the marshway.
<v Alex>别。这是沼泽路。

00:51:19.050 --> 00:51:24.690
<v Jason>I'm going there now because-- because-- there isn't a--
<v Jason>我现在要去那儿，因为——因为——没有一个——

00:51:24.690 --> 00:51:28.790
<v Alex>No, Jason. You don't even know what school is.
<v Alex>不，杰森。你甚至不知道学校是什么。

00:51:28.790 --> 00:51:47.210
<v Vivian Paley>Jason starts to cry. This is also a very emotional moment for me. Alex is right. Jason has interrupted play in an illogical way, in a way that shows he doesn't know that a story is going on. And Alex knows enough to say, you don't know what school is.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森开始哭了。这对我来说也是一个非常情绪化的时刻。亚历克斯是对的。杰森以不合逻辑的方式打断了游戏，以一种表明他不知道故事正在进行的方式。亚历克斯知道足以说，你不知道学校是什么。

00:51:47.210 --> 00:52:15.810
<v Vivian Paley>Which is to say, you don't know what story is. You don't know what fantasy is. You don't know what the most logical relationship between human beings is. Because stories work by the most basic logic, logic a child can understand. It is not too much to say that the only time a child understands everything is in a story.
<v Vivian Paley>也就是，你不知道故事是什么。你不知道幻想是什么。你不知道人类之间最合乎逻辑的关系是什么。因为故事是以最基本的逻辑工作的，孩子能理解的逻辑。说一个孩子唯一一次理解所有事情就是在故事中，这并不为过。

00:52:15.810 --> 00:52:21.030
<v Vivian Paley>As the weeks pass, Samantha's pursuit of Jason grows more inventive.
<v Vivian Paley>随着几周过去，萨曼莎对杰森的追求变得更加有创意。

00:52:21.030 --> 00:52:24.000
<v Samantha>I'm putting you in my story, Jason. Come here and listen.
<v Samantha>我把你放进我的故事里了，杰森。过来听着。

00:52:24.000 --> 00:52:25.450
<v Jason>I don't want to.
<v Jason>我不想。

00:52:25.450 --> 00:52:31.490
<v Samantha>Yes, you have to. Or I won't give you a piece of gum when my daddy buys some.
<v Samantha>是的，你必须听。否则我爸爸买口香糖时，我不给你一块。

00:52:31.490 --> 00:52:52.390
<v Vivian Paley>Jason sits down next to Samantha and watches as she dictates her story. If a teacher had threatened him in that way, he would have withdrawn. When Samantha threatens him, his interest in Samantha's story is heightened. He correctly interprets her warning as a sign of friendship, and the story proves him correct.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森坐在萨曼莎旁边，看着她口述她的故事。如果一位老师那样威胁他，他会退缩。当萨曼莎威胁他时，他对萨曼莎故事的兴趣提高了。他正确地将她的警告解读为友谊的标志，而故事证明他是对的。

00:52:52.390 --> 00:53:05.470
<v Samantha>Once upon a time, there was a little girl. Then a helicopter came. Then the little girl said hello to the helicopter. Then a kitty says hello to the helicopter. Then the kitty, and the girl, and the helicopter are friends.
<v Samantha>从前，有一个小女孩。然后一架直升机来了。然后小女孩对直升机说你好。然后一只小猫对直升机说你好。然后小猫、女孩和直升机成了朋友。

00:53:05.470 --> 00:53:12.360
<v Vivian Paley>After she's done, Jason gathers up a pile of papers for his helicopter drawings.
<v Vivian Paley>她讲完后，杰森收集了一叠纸用来画他的直升机。

00:53:12.360 --> 00:53:15.860
<v Samantha>Don't draw now, Jason. You want to be my husband?
<v Samantha>现在别画了，杰森。你想做我的丈夫吗？

00:53:15.860 --> 00:53:17.380
<v Jason>No, because I'm busy.
<v Jason>不想，因为我很忙。

00:53:17.380 --> 00:53:25.910
<v Samantha>Or the baby, or the cook? Do you want to make a birthday cake? Do you want to be dead, but then you come alive? Remember, you liked it that time?
<v Samantha>或者是宝宝，还是厨师？你想做一个生日蛋糕吗？你想死一次，然后又复活吗？记得吗，你上次很喜欢那样？

00:53:25.910 --> 00:53:34.980
<v Vivian Paley>As Samantha imagines other scenes, she doesn't notice that Jason has stopped drawing, and instead, has cut out a large oval shape.
<v Vivian Paley>当萨曼莎想象其他场景时，她没注意到杰森已经停止了绘画，相反，他剪出了一个大的椭圆形。

00:53:34.980 --> 00:53:40.510
<v Jason>This is your cape, Samantha. Be a queen. I'm the king.
<v Jason>这是你的斗篷，萨曼莎。做个女王。我是国王。

00:53:40.510 --> 00:54:25.510
<v Vivian Paley>But when I glance at their block castle a while later, Jason seems to be the queen's baby. Does Samantha continue to push Jason into the baby role because he appears babyish to her? I doubt it. I've seen too many mature children prefer the baby crib in play. Samantha's motives, I think, are simple and understandable. She likes being the mother and she's fond of Jason. To express her true feelings, she must act the role of his mother. The mother-baby or father-baby relationship spells love most dramatically for many children. By year's end, Samantha will have moved to the big sister-little sister version of the same emotion.
<v Vivian Paley>但当我过一会儿看向他们的积木城堡时，杰森似乎成了女王的宝宝。萨曼莎继续把杰森推向宝宝角色是因为她在她看来杰森像婴儿吗？我对此表示怀疑。我见过太多成熟的孩子更喜欢在玩耍时选择婴儿床。我认为萨曼莎的动机很简单且可以理解。她喜欢当妈妈，而且她很喜欢杰森。为了表达她真实的感情，她必须扮演他妈妈的角色。母婴或父婴关系为许多孩子最戏剧性地表达了爱。到年底，萨曼莎会将同一种情绪演变成大姐-小妹的版本。

00:54:25.510 --> 00:54:35.110
<v Samantha>No, you dope. You [UNINTELLIGIBLE]
<v Samantha>不，你个笨蛋。你 [不清晰]

00:54:35.110 --> 00:55:00.060
<v Vivian Paley>Once changes begin, they happen fast. In one week in April, Jason is a she-baby airplane on Monday, a smaller person looking for Easter eggs on Tuesday, a morning and night boy on Wednesday, an angry fighting person on Thursday. And on Friday, he finds a remarkable new role for his helicopter that takes my breath away in admiration and wonder.
<v Vivian Paley>一旦变化开始，它们发生得很快。四月的一周里，杰森在周一是一个婴儿飞机，周二是一个寻找复活节彩蛋的更小的人，周三是一个晨昏男孩，周四是一个愤怒的战斗者。周五，他为他的直升机找到了一个非凡的新角色，令我感到钦佩和惊叹。

00:55:00.060 --> 00:55:02.410
<v Samantha>Do you want to play, Jason?
<v Samantha>杰森，你想玩吗？

00:55:02.410 --> 00:55:09.500
<v Jason>Yes. You sit there. I made a two seater. Wait. A three seater. One more seat it needs.
<v Jason>是的。你坐在那儿。我做了个双座。等等。三座。还需要一个座位。

00:55:09.500 --> 00:55:10.430
<v Samantha>Why?
<v Samantha>为什么？

00:55:10.430 --> 00:55:17.410
<v Jason>Because I'm going to pick up someone at school, because not anyone will come to pick them up and walk them home.
<v Jason>因为我要在学校接一个人，因为没别人会来接他们并送他们回家。

00:55:17.410 --> 00:55:42.110
<v Vivian Paley>Jason is letting someone else into the helicopter. And when his helicopter finally emerges from its house, he gives it a single task. To take a mother and her child home after school. Underlying this fantasy is a basic fear. No one has arrived to take the school child home. The child is lost at school.
<v Vivian Paley>杰森让别人进入了直升机。当他的直升机最终从房子里出来时，他给了它一个任务。在放学后把妈妈和她的孩子带回家。在这个幻想背后是一种基本的恐惧。没有人来接那个学生回家。那个孩子在学校走丢了。

00:55:42.110 --> 00:55:53.460
<v Jason>I'm going to pick up someone at school. Because not anyone will come to pick them up and walk them home. They're going to hold everyone's hand. One kid's going to hold the other kid's hand.
<v Jason>我要在学校接一个人。因为没别人会来接他们并送他们回家。他们要牵着每个人的手。一个孩子要牵着另一个孩子的手。

00:55:53.460 --> 00:55:54.830
<v Samantha>And I'm the mom, OK?
<v Samantha>我当妈妈，好吗？

00:55:54.830 --> 00:56:00.250
<v Jason>Yes. And everyone, when I get out of school, I'm going to pick them up in my airplane.
<v Jason>是的。每个人，当我从学校出来，我要用我的飞机接他们。

00:56:00.250 --> 00:56:02.710
<v Child>I'll be the kid, Jason. OK?
<v Child>我当那个孩子，杰森。好吗？

00:56:02.710 --> 00:56:31.850
<v Jason>Yes, you're the kid. Hold your mom's hand. I'm flying you home.
<v Jason>是的，你是那个孩子。牵着你妈妈的手。我送你们回家。

00:56:31.850 --> 00:57:00.460
<v Ira Glass>Well, Vivian Paley's book, The Boy Who Would Be a Helicopter, from which this was adapted, is published by Harvard University Press. The children's dialogue was reenacted from classroom transcripts by Joe Robinson, Jason [? Bogart, ?] [? Aisha ?] [? Harmon, ?] [? Matt ?] [? Kady, ?] Anna [? Klumsky ?] Beth [? Iams, ?] and Jenny [? Banachefski, ?] who played Jason. Funding came from the University of Chicago's William Benton Broadcast Project, Claudia [? Daily, ?] and the late Louis Friedman, supervising producers.
<v Ira Glass>嗯，薇薇安·佩利的《想成为直升机的男孩》一书，从中改编了这一节，由哈佛大学出版社出版。儿童对话由乔·罗宾逊、杰森 [?博加特?]、[?艾莎?] [?哈蒙?]、[?马特?] [?卡迪?]、安娜 [?克鲁姆斯基?]、贝丝 [?伊姆斯?] 和饰演杰森的詹妮 [?巴纳切夫斯基?] 根据课堂记录重现。资金来自芝加哥大学的威廉·本顿广播项目、克劳迪娅 [?戴利?] 和已故的刘易斯·弗里德曼，监制制作人。

00:57:00.460 --> 00:57:15.730
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Susan Burton and myself, with Alex Blumberg, Blue Chevigny, and Julie Snyder. Contributing editors Paul Tough, Jack Hitt, Margy Rochlin, Alix Spiegel, Nancy Updike, and conciliary Sarah Vowell. Production help from Todd Bachmann and Eric [? Harverston. ?] Musical help from Mr. John Connors.
<v Ira Glass>我们的节目今天由苏珊·伯顿和我自己制作，还有亚历克斯·布卢姆伯格、布鲁·切维尼和朱莉·斯奈德。特约编辑保罗·塔夫、杰克·希特、玛吉·罗克林、阿利克斯·斯皮格尔、南希·厄普代克和顾问莎拉·沃威尔。制作帮助来自托德·巴赫曼和埃里克 [?哈弗斯顿?]。音乐帮助来自约翰·康纳斯先生。

00:57:15.730 --> 00:57:19.770
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:57:19.770 --> 00:57:41.710
<v Ira Glass>To buy a cassette of this program, call us here at WBEZ in Chicago, 312-832-3380. Or you know, you can listen to most of our programs for free on the internet at our website at www.thislife.org. Thanks to Elizabeth Meister, who runs the site. This week a photo on there of Dare Wright. This American Life is distributed by Public Radio International.
<v Ira Glass>如需购买本节目的磁带，请致电芝加哥WBEZ，电话312-832-3380。或者你知道，你可以免费在网上收听我们大部分节目，网址是www.thislife.org。感谢运行该网站的伊丽莎白·迈斯特。本周上面有戴尔·赖特的照片。《美国生活》由国际公共广播电台发行。

00:57:41.710 --> 00:58:09.790
<v Ira Glass>[FUNDING  CREDITS]
<v Ira Glass>[资金赞助鸣谢]

00:58:09.790 --> 00:58:14.340
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ management oversight by Torey Malatia, who can be found every morning in his office--
<v Ira Glass>WBEZ管理监督由托里·马拉蒂亚负责，他每天早晨都可以在他的办公室里被找到——

00:58:14.340 --> 00:58:19.820
<v Jean Nathan>Seated on the floor, surrounded by bears, telling bear stories in various bear voices.
<v Jean Nathan>坐在地板上，被熊群包围着，用各种熊的声音讲熊的故事。

00:58:19.820 --> 00:58:55.170
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira Glass. Back next week with more stories of This American Life .
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯。下周回到《美国生活》的更多故事中。

00:58:55.170 --> 00:59:00.170
<v Announcer>PRI. Public Radio International.
<v Announcer>PRI。国际公共广播电台。
